<h2 id="workspace-layout">Choose a comfortable workspace layout</h2>
<p>The eight public writing and conversion tools start with <strong>Full-width panels</strong>. The editor comes first, then the reading preview and export controls. Previews use the page's scrolling, so long articles do not need a second scrolling area inside the page. The three-step bar brings review and export into view without changing the writing.</p>
<p>Open <strong>Layout options</strong>, then choose <strong>Side by side on wide screens</strong> under Workspace layout to compare the editor and reading preview. On screens up to 850 pixels wide, either choice uses one column and previews remain in the page flow. In a wide comparison layout, focus the reading preview to scroll its bounded reading area with a keyboard.</p>
<p><strong>Edit article</strong> in the step bar stays nearby while you read. <strong>Back to editing</strong> is also available below the reading preview and at the start of the export controls once an article is present. These shortcuts focus the article body and keep its cursor or selected passage. They do not reopen the Word picker or Google Docs paste box. A pending article import takes precedence: apply or clear it first. File operations temporarily disable these shortcuts.</p>
<p>Layout changes leave article fields, private notes, selected text, checkpoints, and export approval as they are. Editing writing still clears approval. The layout choice lasts only in the open tab and is excluded from backups and exports. Reopening the tool starts with full-width panels. Browser recovery is separate, optional, and off by default.</p>
<h2 id="guided-workflow">Move from writing to review to export</h2>
<p>In the Word, Google Docs, and HTML source converters, inspect the import preview and acknowledge its conversion notices. Choose <strong>Import and continue to export</strong> to apply the reviewed article and open your selected destination. Choose <strong>Import and edit</strong> to work on the article first. Replacing existing writing still asks for confirmation and keeps a checkpoint of valid previous writing. Both choices clear export approval. Continuing keeps your notes-panel visibility, selected destination, and publishing-profile preferences; it does not copy, download, or publish. Inserting into an existing article returns to the editor and its selected location.</p>
<p>When writing or pasting directly into the article editor, add the body, read its preview, then choose the destination and review its options before checking export approval. A separate title is optional for WordPress blocks and article Markdown. HTML and Word require a title only when you choose to include it. Page metadata can stay empty for these article exports. Open <strong>Format text or find passages</strong> below the editor when you need those tools.</p>
<p>The three-step bar stays nearby as you move through the workspace. Add writing opens your input; Word and Google Docs tools offer their own file or paste starting point. Review opens the reading preview and brings it into view. Export takes you to destination choices and the separate review checkbox. The highlighted step records navigation, not completion or a quality score.</p>
<p>After editing an article, choose <strong>Review this article</strong> below the editor, then <strong>Continue to WordPress</strong>, <strong>Continue to HTML source</strong>, or <strong>Continue to Word</strong> below the preview. An unfinished article import takes precedence in these shortcuts: inspect its pending conversion and apply or clear it before using them for the current article. Navigation keeps writing, source notes, selections, export choices, and approval. It never copies, downloads, or publishes.</p>
<p>The bar reports pending imports, file operations, and your current approval choice. Typing changes no focus or scroll position automatically. File-first conversion previews and explicit navigation bring the relevant section into view. The bar becomes static in short windows so it takes less space while you work. The navigation state stays in this tab and is excluded from backups and exports. Save a private backup before leaving.</p>
<h2 id="keep-current-draft">Use the same draft in another format</h2>
<p>When you have work in a public writer or converter, open <strong>Convert</strong> and use <strong>Use this draft</strong> to choose WordPress blocks, Markdown article, HTML source, or Word document. These choices keep you in the current workspace with your writing, private source notes, selected passage, checkpoints, and unsaved editing preparation. Choosing a different destination clears export approval so you can review its options; choosing the same destination keeps approval. WordPress linked-image consent resets when you leave that destination.</p>
<p>A pending import stays available for review with its source and conversion choices. Apply or clear it before exporting. File operations temporarily pause destination changes. <strong>Edit current draft</strong> returns to the article without opening a new converter or replacing writing.</p>
<p><strong>Start another conversion</strong> opens a fresh converter in a new tab while work is present. Converter links in the page also show <strong>(new tab)</strong>. Your current work stays in the original tab and is not copied into the new one. Examples, Templates, Help, and home links open separate tabs from a workspace. Keep the original tab open while you use them.</p>
<h2 id="leave-and-return">Save before closing, then review what you restore</h2>
<p>Choose <strong>Save options</strong> to keep a private backup or explicitly enable browser recovery. Browser recovery is off by default and keeps one unencrypted copy on this browser, including private source notes. It excludes pending imports, unsaved editing preparation, collections, checkpoints, and export preferences. Pending preparation stays only in the open tab. See <a href="#browser-recovery">browser recovery</a> for its limits and saved-copy controls.</p>
<p>When a browser copy is available after returning, inspect it and compare its fields with the current workspace before restoring. Nothing is restored automatically. Check the actual save status and time; an open tab, an article export, and an unsaved import are not recovery copies.</p>
